{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>הֲלֹא זֶה</b>, etc. There was no need to mention it, since this man — namely the one who placed the goblet in Benjamin's sack — for this reason he had no need to mention it, and the matter was self-evident to him, that it was about the goblet he was speaking. And the intent of his words was to inform them of the reason for their being detected in the theft immediately and at once, for this would indicate that the matter was the work of their own hands, which they had planned. Therefore they said, ״הֲלֹא זֶה אֲשֶׁר יִשְׁתֶּה אֲדֹנִי בּוֹ״ — meaning, regularly, and from this perspective it is an object he is accustomed to use, and from this they knew immediately of its theft. And his saying <b>וְהוּא נַחֵשׁ יְנַחֵשׁ</b> comes to give a reason for his words in this manner, in straightforwardness, that they stole it and he did not suspect anyone else; therefore he said ״נַחֵשׁ יְנַחֵשׁ״ — meaning, נַחֵשׁ — what is before him; יְנַחֵשׁ — what is destined to be, and through it he divined and knew that they stole it. Or perhaps it is for this reason that they paid attention to it after their departure." "<b>הֲרֵעֹתֶם אֲשֶׁר עֲשִׂיתֶם.</b> It is difficult: after he said, ״לָמָּה שִׁלַּמְתֶּם רָעָה תַּחַת טוֹבָה״, what place is there to say <b>הֲרֵעֹתֶם</b>, etc.? For is there any evil in the world like repaying evil for good!" "Or perhaps he intended to refute the proof from the contrary, drawn from the fact that they returned the forgotten silver in their sacks — for this man, to whom they had disclosed that they had returned with them the silver found with them, and if so he would make a fortiori argument: ״הֵן כֶּסֶף״ etc. ״וְאֵיךְ נִגְנוֹב״, as they answered afterward. Therefore he said <b>הֲרֵעוֹתֶם אֲשֶׁר עֲשִׂיתֶם</b> — for by this you concealed that strong presumption which I had established concerning your integrity through the deed you did in returning the silver, and your presumption was worsened, that they are not men of truth, and I attribute the deed to coincidence, and it is among the things that even thieves do, and does not proceed from the discipline of reason." "<b>Further</b>, it may intend in his saying <b>הֲרֵעֹתֶם אֲשֶׁר עֲשִׂיתֶם</b> — meaning, in the return of the silver — for through the very act of this thing they revealed their intent, that in returning the silver they did this, on the contrary, out of wickedness, to establish them as men of faithfulness, so that through this they would trust them and find opportunity to steal precious things more valuable than the returned silver — for the goblet is held to be of greater value than the silver they returned with them."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 44, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:48:36.542749378Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}